A secret in a pipeline is not secure because it is hidden in the logs. It is secure because it cannot be reached where it should not be.

A short pass worth doing:

- No secrets in code, in pipeline files, or in container image layers. A secret removed in a later layer is still in the image history.
- Scope the secret to the job that needs it, not to the whole pipeline. A deploy job does not need the same credentials as a test job.
- Prefer short-lived credentials over long-lived keys. A key that lives for a year is a year of exposure if it leaks.
- Rotate on a schedule, and rotate immediately after anyone with access leaves or after a suspected exposure. Rotation that only happens after an incident is not a control.
- Log that a secret was used, never the value itself. If the value can be found in a log, the secret store did not protect it.

Pipelines move secrets to more places than a human ever would. That is exactly why the scoping and rotation rules have to be stricter there, not looser.
